>
  <article class="content">
    <p class="eyebrow">Window direction guide</p>
    <h1>East-Facing Window Plants: A Morning-Sun Placement Guide</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      An east-facing window usually gives plants gentle direct sun in the morning and bright indirect light later.
      It is one of the easiest apartment exposures, but distance, outdoor shade, and summer heat still matter.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Fast answer:</strong> Start flowering plants and succulents within 1–2 ft (30–60 cm) of clear glass.
      Start most tropical foliage plants 1–4 ft (30–120 cm) away. Move them closer if new growth becomes small,
      pale, or widely spaced.
    </div>

    <h2>Best plants by distance from an east-facing window</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Starting zone</th><th>Plants to try</th><th>Main risk</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Windowsill to 1 ft</td><td>Hoya, jade plant, echeveria, aloe, herbs</td><td>Hot glass or sudden summer exposure</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1–3 ft</td><td>Monstera, rubber plant, bird of paradise, pilea</td><td>Leaves touching cold glass in winter</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3–6 ft</td><td>Pothos, heartleaf philodendron, peace lily, calathea</td><td>Light falling too quickly in a deep room</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Beyond 6 ft</td><td>ZZ plant, snake plant, cast iron plant</td><td>Survival without healthy new growth</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      These distances are starting points, not guarantees. A balcony above the window, a tree, insect screen, tinted
      glass, or a narrow courtyard can turn an apparently bright east window into a low-light location. Use the
      <a href="/light-meter/">window light estimator</a> when the view is obstructed.
    </p>

    <h2>How to place a plant in five minutes</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Check the spot between 8 a.m. and 11 a.m. on a clear day.</li>
      <li>Notice whether direct sun reaches the leaves, the floor only, or never enters the room.</li>
      <li>Check whether the plant can see a broad area of open sky from its final position.</li>
      <li>Choose a starting distance from the table, then keep the pot there for two to four weeks.</li>
      <li>Judge the placement by new growth rather than old leaves that formed elsewhere.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Plants that benefit from the morning sun</h2>
    <h3>Hoya</h3>
    <p>
      A hoya can use several hours of gentle morning sun and often grows or flowers better close to the window.
      Acclimate a plant bought from a dim shop over a week instead of placing it immediately against the glass.
    </p>
    <h3>Monstera and rubber plant</h3>
    <p>
      Both prefer a bright position. Put them where the morning sun reaches the plant or just outside the direct beam.
      Rotate the pot a quarter turn every week or two if growth leans strongly toward the window.
    </p>
    <h3>Pothos and heartleaf philodendron</h3>
    <p>
      These adaptable vines can sit farther into the room, but adaptation is not the same as thriving. Longer gaps
      between leaves and smaller new leaves mean the plant should move closer.
    </p>
    <h3>Succulents</h3>
    <p>
      An unobstructed east window is useful but may still be marginal for sun-hungry succulents, especially in winter.
      Keep them on the sill, watch for stretching, and add a grow light if compact growth cannot be maintained.
    </p>

    <h2>Seasonal adjustments</h2>
    <p>
      Summer sun rises farther northeast and reaches some east windows early and intensely. Winter days are shorter,
      the sun is lower, and buildings may block more of it. Moving a plant 1–2 ft closer for winter is often more useful
      than changing the watering schedule alone. Keep foliage from touching cold glass.
    </p>

    <h2>Signs the placement is wrong</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>First adjustment</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Small or widely spaced new leaves</td><td>Too little usable light</td><td>Move 1–2 ft closer</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bleached tan patches on the window side</td><td>Sudden direct-sun exposure</td><td>Move back or filter midday spill</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pot stays wet for many days</td><td>Low light, cool soil, or dense mix</td><td>Pause watering and reassess the spot</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Plant leans sharply toward the glass</td><td>One-sided light</td><td>Rotate regularly or move closer</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>East-facing versus north-facing windows</h2>
    <p>
      East windows normally offer more usable energy because direct morning sun reaches the room. North windows are
      steadier but dimmer in the northern hemisphere. If your apartment has both, reserve the east window for plants
      that need active growth or flowering and use the north window for tolerant foliage. See the
      <a href="/north-facing-window-plants/">north-facing window guide</a> for that lower-light setup.
    </p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <h3>Is an east-facing window bright indirect light?</h3>
    <p>
      It usually provides direct morning sun followed by bright indirect light. Obstructions and distance can reduce
      it substantially, so the direction alone does not define the final light level.
    </p>
    <h3>Can a monstera sit directly in an east-facing window?</h3>
    <p>
      Usually yes after gradual acclimation, provided the glass does not become very hot and the leaves are not pressed
      against it. Watch the newest leaves for bleaching or crisp patches.
    </p>
    <h3>How far can a plant be from an east-facing window?</h3>
    <p>
      Many foliage plants can start 1–4 ft away. Past 6 ft, room shape and outdoor obstructions matter more than compass
      direction. Use the <a href="/plant-distance-calculator/">plant distance calculator</a> for a tailored starting zone.
    </p>
  </article>
